 "exercise": {
  "id": "pqp03-ex09",
  "chapter": 3,
  "number": "3.9",
  "title": "Zero eats everything",
  "level": 3,
  "statement": "PQP §3.4.2: if part of a process is impossible, the whole process is — a zero process absorbs everything it is composed with (equations (3.27)). In the Playground the two sides of an equation must have the same wires, so \"zero\" here is the number 0 beside a plain wire.\n\n- (a) Prove the given equation in **at least two steps**, naming each rule. (Number nodes: a label that reads as a number is that number.)\n- (b) On paper: PQP Exercise 3.40 asks you to show that if $f \\approx 0$ then $f = 0$, in a theory with no zero-divisors. Prove it, and say where you use that λ and μ in Def. 3.39 are non-zero.",
  "done": "The Exercise panel shows ✓ Solved, and you have a proof of (b).",
